> But for this recommendation letter, he has a different proposed project. Here it is in its entirety:
> ```
> Pricing the Path Inland: What Landowners Will Accept to Let
> Delaware's Marshes Migrate
> Applicant
> Muhammad Taky Tahmid
> tahmid@udel.edu | 302-533-0260 | 272 The Green #111, Newark, DE 19716
> Mentor team
> George Parsons, Academic Mentor
> E. I. du Pont Professor, University of Delaware. Guides the discrete choice experiment and my
> development at the boundary of valuation and conservation policy.
> Kristi Lieske, Primary Practitioner Mentor
> Ocean Planner, Delaware Coastal Programs, Delaware Department of Natural Resources and
> Environmental Control. Connects the research to DNREC programs, data, staff, and agency use.
> Sarah Bouboulis, Practitioner Co-Mentor
> Delaware Coastal Programs, Delaware Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Control.
> Advises on program context, agency resources, and application of the findings.
> References
> James Rising
> School of Marine Science and Policy, University of Delaware
> Kyle Davis
> Department of Geography and Spatial Sciences, University of Delaware
> Title Page | 1
> Abstract
> Delaware's tidal marshes are losing elevation relative to the sea, and where they cannot keep pace their
> persistence depends on moving inland. DNREC's marsh migration model, released in July 2025, identifies
> 21,449 acres as highly suitable for migration under a four-foot scenario. The model describes physical
> suitability but does not identify which landowners would participate in conservation or what participation
> would cost. Much of the suitable area intersects privately held land. Delaware's 2026-2030 Wetland
> Program Plan calls for increasing wetland acreage but does not specify the instruments or costs required
> to secure migration opportunities on private land.
> Field et al. (2017) surveyed landowners in Connecticut's migration zone and found that participation
> varied sharply by contractual arrangement: 6.9 percent were likely to accept a permanent conservation
> easement, while 27 percent preferred future interest agreements. They measured intentions on rating
> scales without attaching payments, so the cost of securing migration land remains unestimated.
> This project estimates what Delaware landowners would accept, under which arrangements, and tests
> whether parcel-specific information about projected inundation changes their responses. I will first
> determine which conservation arrangements Delaware agencies or partners could legally and
> administratively implement. I will then contact owners of parcels intersecting the migration zone with a
> mailed invitation to a survey containing an alternative-specific choice experiment over the feasible
> arrangements. The survey will randomize whether respondents see a projection for their own land. This
> information treatment is the primary causal design; owners' perceived timelines are a secondary, post-
> treatment measure.
> The work produces three outputs: a parcel and ownership frame for the migration zone, stated-preference
> estimates that include the information treatment, and decision scenarios for DNREC. The parcel analysis
> will also quantify how much suitable land is already subject to agricultural preservation restrictions, an
> institutional constraint that Delaware has not yet measured in relation to marsh migration.
> Background & Context
> Problem, scientific context, and conservation relevance
> Tidal marshes persist under rising seas by building elevation in place or by migrating inland. Where
> accretion falls behind, migration is what preserves marsh area. Schieder et al. (2018) found that across the
> mapped quarter of Chesapeake Bay marshland, 94 square kilometers were lost to shoreline erosion while
> 101 were created by upland drowning. Kirwan et al. (2016) show migration rates responding more
> Research Proposal | 1
> sharply to accelerating sea-level rise than erosion rates where marshes are unobstructed. Where
> topography, armoring or development obstructs it, marsh area is not replaced.
> Delaware has mapped where migration is physically possible. DNREC released an updated model in July
> 2025, developed during 2023 by its Wetland Monitoring and Assessment Program with Delaware Coastal
> Programs, revising a 2017 version. It scores land from 12, highly suitable, down to 0 under two-foot,
> four-foot and seven-foot scenarios. Under four feet the model identifies 21,449 highly suitable acres:
> 9,418 in Sussex County, 8,482 in Kent and 3,549 in New Castle. The four-foot layer is published on
> FirstMap Delaware.
> Saltmarsh sparrow populations are declining rapidly. The Atlantic Coast Joint Venture's Saltmarsh
> Sparrow Conservation Plan (Hartley & Weldon, 2020) reports the species falling at 9 percent per year
> since 1998, from 212,000 individuals to a projected 28,215 by 2020, with fewer than 10,000 expected by
> 2031 if those rates hold. Range-wide, 48 percent of nests produced no nestling between 2011 and 2015.
> Delaware's share is roughly 4,100 birds. The species is not federally listed; the Center for Biological
> Diversity petitioned in April 2024 and sued in February 2026 over the Service's failure to make the
> required initial determination. Delaware's marshes also support diamondback terrapin and provide nursery
> habitat for blue crab and finfish (Jivoff & Able, 2003).
> Land conversion is already underway. Mondal et al. (2023) mapped 8,096 hectares of Delmarva farmland
> converting to marsh between 2011 and 2017, and estimated annual losses of $39.4 million to $107.5
> million on at-risk farmland within 200 meters of visible salt patches. Molino et al. (2026) find roughly
> 25,000 acres of farmland lost across the Chesapeake and Delaware Bay watersheds between 1984 and
> 2022, with marsh advancing faster on agricultural land than on forest. These studies show that farmland
> makes up a substantial part of the land now converting to marsh.
> The remaining uncertainty concerns landowners. Physical migration mapping is widely available:
> NOAA's Sea Level Rise Viewer covers the contiguous United States, and regional products derive from
> it, including the USGS Chesapeake Bay dataset (Defne et al., 2025). Whether landowners will permit
> migration, and on what terms, is far less well understood. Field et al. (2017) established that participation
> depends heavily on the arrangement offered, with permanent easements attracting few owners and future
> interest agreements considerably more. Burman et al. (2023) showed that migration-corridor parcels in
> Rhode Island are disproportionately private and more highly assessed than parcels holding current marsh.
> Smith et al. (2026), a team including Dayer, extended this to agricultural landowners' perceptions.
> Existing studies describe preferences over arrangements but do not estimate what those arrangements
> would cost.
> Research Proposal | 2
> Rationale and Innovation
> Delaware now has a migration model and a new Wetland Program Plan, but neither provides the
> landowner information or cost estimates needed for implementation. The model was released in July
> 2025, and its projections have not yet been communicated to the landowners they concern. The 2026-
> 2030 Wetland Program Plan commits the Division of Watershed Stewardship to increasing wetland
> acreage but does not identify the instruments or costs needed to secure migration opportunities on private
> land. A supporting document for the Delaware Ocean and Bay Plan, prepared for DNREC Coastal
> Programs by the University of Maryland Environmental Finance Center and the Trust for Public Land
> (2026), asks how much unprotected land is both salt-affected and highly suitable for migration, and what
> incentive payments Delaware should consider beyond federal programs.
> This project adds three elements to existing work. First, attaching payments to each arrangement permits
> estimation of willingness to accept, while earlier studies ranked stated interest without identifying cost.
> Second, random assignment of a parcel-specific projection identifies how that information changes stated
> participation. Third, the choice task evaluates agreements designed for land that may remain in production
> for years before marsh reaches it, including options that Field et al. did not price.
> The instrument feasibility review comes before the choice experiment. It will determine which
> arrangements Delaware agencies or conservation partners could legally hold, fund and administer.
> Possible arrangements include future interest agreements, term agreements, conservation easements and
> purchase, but the survey will include only those that survive this review. This sequencing avoids asking
> owners to price an arrangement that could not be offered.
> Agricultural preservation easements present a separate institutional constraint. Delaware Code Title 3,
> Chapter 9 limits land under these easements to agricultural and related uses; the permitted-use provisions
> do not expressly contemplate conversion to tidal marsh. Release is governed by the limited process in
> §917 after at least twenty-five years. Selection is not based on price alone: §914(b) directs the Foundation
> to consider the §908(b) criteria, including agricultural productivity, together with agricultural value and
> the discount offered. The parcel frame will measure how much of the migration zone is subject to these
> easements. I will then consult the Agricultural Lands Preservation Foundation about how it interprets the
> overlap, including whether its authority under §914(c) to designate acquisition priority areas has any
> relevance. The proposal does not assume that this provision resolves the conflict.
> The work departs substantially from my doctoral research. Two dissertation chapters are climate
> econometrics, covering agricultural wages under in-kind compensation and the weighting of empirical
> and process-based crop models. The third values recreational crabbing and clamming among people who
> Research Proposal | 3
> had already chosen to participate, using a survey with a single randomized bid. The proposed project
> concerns landowners facing substantial financial stakes, uses an alternative-specific choice experiment,
> and evaluates the effect of an information intervention on their choices.
> Transformative Potential
> The project would give Delaware a defensible range for the cost of securing migration opportunities
> under the arrangements that prove feasible. Those estimates could inform agency budgeting, future
> funding requests and discussions with conservation partners.
> The information experiment also examines how landowners respond to parcel-level climate risk
> information. Coastal agencies are moving toward this form of communication, but evidence about
> recipients' responses remains limited. A causal estimate of whether personalized projections increase
> willingness to participate or harden resistance would apply to settings beyond marsh migration.
> The approach could also be adapted elsewhere when parcel records and comparable migration layers are
> available. NOAA's Sea Level Rise Viewer supplies a common starting point, while each state would need
> to establish its own legal instruments, ownership data and agency pathway.
> Pathway to Application
> The intended users are DNREC's Division of Watershed Stewardship, which holds the Wetland Program
> Plan, and Delaware Coastal Programs, which co-produced the migration model and commissioned the
> valuation strategy that poses these questions. Kristi Lieske, Ocean Planner with Coastal Programs, is the
> primary practitioner mentor and has confirmed that the migration model contains no social science
> component. Sarah Bouboulis of Delaware Coastal Programs is the practitioner co-mentor and will advise
> on program context, relevant agency resources, and application of the findings.
> Each output corresponds to a question DNREC has already identified. The parcel frame measures
> unprotected, salt-affected, and highly suitable acreage and will be delivered as a spatial layer. The
> willingness-to-accept estimates address the cost of incentives beyond federal programs. The decision
> scenarios combine these results to show, under stated assumptions, how much acreage a given budget
> might secure through each feasible arrangement and where agricultural preservation restrictions reduce
> the available area.
> No current Delaware program has yet been verified as able to fund inland agreements with agricultural
> landowners for marsh migration. The feasibility review will identify which agencies or conservation
> partners could hold and administer each arrangement before the survey presents it to landowners.
> Research Proposal | 4
> I have worked with DNREC for more than a year across two projects: an assessment of data gaps for
> marine spatial planning and a valuation of recreational shellfishing built on a survey of Delaware license
> holders conducted with DNREC's cooperation. Those relationships provide an established route for
> DNREC staff to shape the research questions and review the resulting decision scenarios.
> Origin Story
> George Parsons sits on my dissertation committee and is my coauthor on its third chapter, a contingent-
> behavior valuation of recreational crabbing and clamming estimated from a survey of Delaware
> shellfishing license holders. That chapter taught me how to field a large stated-preference instrument in
> Delaware and what a state agency does with the resulting numbers.
> I met Kristi Lieske through DNREC's assessment of data gaps for marine spatial planning and worked
> with her again on shellfishing valuation. On 5 May 2026, I presented that work on the Regional Ocean
> Economy panel at the Mid-Atlantic Ocean Forum in Virginia Beach, convened by the Mid-Atlantic
> Regional Council on the Ocean. Kristi moderated the session. It was my first presentation outside a
> university, and hearing practitioners debate what they could do with a finding sharpened my interest in
> research built around an agency decision.
> The project took its present shape in September 2026. I had read DNREC's published priorities and
> brought Kristi a proposal built around marsh migration under sea-level rise. She explained that DNREC
> already maintains a physical migration model, built by colleagues in her division, but that the model
> contains no landowner or economic component. That conversation redirected the proposal toward the
> decisions DNREC still could not make from the map alone.
> I will design and lead the parcel frame, instrument feasibility review, survey and experiment, estimation,
> and translation into decision scenarios. My working relationships are with Delaware Coastal Programs
> through Kristi Lieske and Sarah Bouboulis, and with the Wetland Monitoring and Assessment Program,
> which built the model. I will consult the Agricultural Lands Preservation Foundation about the overlap
> with protected farmland. The ecological component will use DNREC's existing suitability classifications
> and wetland assessments rather than construct an independent habitat-ranking model. My contribution is
> environmental economics, spatial analysis, and policy design.
> Statement of Objectives
> 1. Build a parcel and ownership frame for Delaware's marsh migration zone, classifying parcels by
> suitability, protection status, ownership, land use and salt intrusion exposure, and quantifying the overlap
> with agricultural preservation easements.
> Research Proposal | 5
> 2. Establish which conservation instruments are legally and administratively available in Delaware for
> securing migration land from private owners, through documentary review and practitioner consultation.
> 3. Estimate landowner willingness to accept across those instruments, and estimate the causal effect of
> parcel-specific inundation information on stated participation.
> 4. Translate the estimates into decision scenarios for DNREC, stating the assumptions under which stated
> preferences would approximate realized participation.
> Approaches & Methods
> Parcel and ownership frame
> DNREC's suitability surface is joined to county parcel boundaries by zonal overlay, so each parcel carries
> the share of its area scoring highly suitable. Protection status is assembled from recorded conservation
> easements, public holdings and Agricultural Preservation District enrollments. DNREC's existing
> suitability classifications and wetland condition assessments provide the ecological measures; the project
> does not create an independent habitat ranking. The analysis retains DNREC's stated sea-level-rise
> scenarios and their uncertainty instead of assigning each parcel a precise year of transition from elevation
> alone.
> The completed frame will establish the size of the contact population during the first quarter. Survey
> design, power and mailing costs will then be based on that observed population.
> Instrument feasibility review
> Before designing the choice task, I will establish which arrangements Delaware can offer through an
> agency or conservation partner. The review covers statutory authority, which entity could hold the
> interest, how each arrangement interacts with agricultural preservation easements, and whether an
> existing or plausible funding source could support it. It draws on Delaware Code, program documents,
> and consultation with DNREC, Department of Agriculture staff and the Agricultural Lands Preservation
> Foundation. Only arrangements that pass this review will enter the survey.
> Survey design and the information experiment
> After constructing the ownership frame, I will determine whether the available budget and statistical
> power support contacting every identifiable owner or require a stratified sample. In either design,
> incomplete ownership records, undeliverable addresses and nonresponse may produce selection. Owners
> with stronger conservation preferences may be especially likely to reply.
> Research Proposal | 6
> Contact will follow four waves based on Dillman's tailored design method: a prenotice letter, a main
> invitation carrying a small prepaid incentive and web link, a reminder, and a final invitation enclosing a
> paper questionnaire. Treatment assignment occurs before contact. Paper packets will be generated
> individually so treatment respondents receive the same parcel-specific map shown online, while control
> respondents receive the same general information used in the web instrument. The paper option reduces
> coverage error among owners who do not use a web form. Mailing, incentive, and customized-printing
> costs will be checked against the fellowship budget once the ownership frame is complete.
> The choice task is alternative-specific and will include only the arrangements retained after the feasibility
> review. Each arrangement carries attributes that apply to it. A purchase option may vary in one-time
> payment and timing of transfer; a future interest agreement in payment, triggering condition and interim
> use; a term agreement in annual payment, duration and permitted use; and a conservation easement in
> one-time payment, retained rights and the portion of the parcel covered. Respondents can decline all
> arrangements. I will develop the experimental design with Parsons and refine it through cognitive
> interviews with approximately twelve landowners. The number of alternatives, blocks and tasks will be
> finalized after the ownership frame and power calculations are complete.
> The information treatment randomizes, at the individual level, whether a respondent receives a projection
> for their own parcel or general information about marsh migration in Delaware. This randomization is the
> primary causal design and the treatment effect on stated participation is the principal estimand.
> Respondents' own beliefs about their timeline are recorded, but they are measured after treatment and are
> not randomly assigned, so they enter as a secondary descriptive measure and as a dimension of
> heterogeneity, not as an identified mechanism.
> Secondary outcomes include stated intent to undertake defensive measures. The study records intentions
> and does not observe subsequent construction. For parcels inland of the shoreline, relevant responses may
> include ditching, berms and drainage alteration. The survey will ask only about measures that are
> physically relevant to each respondent's parcel.
> Nonresponse and inference
> Parcel records allow respondents and nonrespondents to be compared on parcel size, suitability, assessed
> value, land use, protection status, and owner type. I will report these comparisons and present weighted
> and unweighted estimates when observed differences are material. Weighting cannot correct selection on
> unobserved characteristics, and the interpretation will retain that limitation. Once the ownership frame is
> built, I will calculate minimum detectable effects and mailing costs before finalizing treatment allocation
> Research Proposal | 7
> and the choice design. If the projected sample cannot support the full design, I will reduce the number of
> alternatives or planned heterogeneity analyses before fielding.
> Ethics and disclosure
> Telling an owner that their property may become marsh could affect decisions about the land, its
> perceived value, or willingness to speak with the state. I will develop disclosure language with DNREC
> and submit the complete survey protocol to the University of Delaware IRB. Each projection will identify
> its scenario and uncertainty and will not be presented as a prediction of a specific outcome or date. The
> protocol will determine whether control respondents should be offered, rather than automatically sent,
> their parcel-specific projection after the survey. Parcel-level responses will remain confidential and
> results will be reported in aggregate because willingness to accept is commercially sensitive.
> Estimation
> Mixed logit models will estimate willingness to accept within the feasible arrangements and recover
> preference heterogeneity where the achieved sample supports it. Latent class models will be treated as an
> extension that depends on sample size and class stability. The randomized information treatment will be
> estimated on stated participation, willingness to accept and defensive intentions. All estimates are stated
> preferences. Decision scenarios will show how conclusions change under alternative assumptions about
> hypothetical bias, nonresponse and actual program availability.
> Feasibility
> My third dissertation chapter draws on a statewide survey of Delaware license holders, with final
> contingent-behavior samples of 3,531 crabbers and 1,227 clammers. I helped design, pretest, program,
> and field the survey and worked through the resulting response and nonresponse issues. The survey was
> conducted through the University of Delaware with the cooperation of the Delaware Department of
> Natural Resources and Environmental Control and Institutional Review Board approval, with George
> Parsons as coauthor. The proposed ownership frame is not yet known, so its size, mailing cost and
> statistical power will be established before the design is finalized. Alternative-specific choice experiment
> design is new to me and is one reason Parsons' mentorship is central to the project.
> Timeline
> Year one, first quarter: assemble the parcel frame, obtain DNREC layers and parcel records, and complete
> the first stage of the instrument feasibility review. Second quarter: calculate power and mailing costs,
> select the feasible arrangements, design and cognitively test the survey with Parsons, finalize disclosure
> Research Proposal | 8
> language with DNREC, and submit the complete protocol for IRB review. Third and fourth quarters: field
> the survey across four waves, conditional on IRB approval.
> Year two, first quarter: estimation and nonresponse analysis. Second quarter: decision scenarios
> developed with DNREC, parcel frame delivered. Third quarter: consultation with the Agricultural Lands
> Preservation Foundation on the overlap finding. Fourth quarter: manuscript submission and final delivery
> to DNREC.
> One manuscript is planned within the fellowship. The feasibility review and agricultural preservation
> overlap may support later work, but they are not separate fellowship deliverables. Fellowship retreats are
> included in the schedule, with survey fielding and analysis placed between them where possible.
> Anticipated Results
> The parcel frame will establish how much of the 21,449 highly suitable acres is unprotected, privately
> held and salt-affected, and how much is subject to agricultural preservation easements. This will show
> whether the two state priorities overlap and how much land may be unavailable under current restrictions.
> Following Field et al. (2017), I expect participation to vary by arrangement. The experiment will estimate
> those differences while accounting for payment timing, retained rights and other terms that are specific to
> each feasible arrangement. The results will show whether owners are more receptive to agreements that
> allow continued use before marsh migration occurs.
> The information treatment has no expected sign. Personalized projections may raise willingness to
> participate by making a distant risk concrete, or they may lower participation and increase stated interest
> in defensive measures. Before fielding, the power analysis will establish which treatment effects the
> available frame can detect. Either direction would inform how DNREC approaches parcel-level risk
> communication.
> The decision scenarios will state what a given budget might secure under each available arrangement,
> with the assumptions required to move from stated to realized participation set out explicitly. Where those
> assumptions cannot be supported, the scenario will say so.
