> Budget and Timeline Narrative
> David H Smith Conservation Research Fellowship
> The project uses the full $45,000 research allowance, divided evenly between the two fellowship years.
> Most of the budget supports the parcel and ownership frame and the four-wave landowner survey,
> including its information experiment. The remaining funds cover analysis and preparation of decision
> scenarios for the Delaware Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Control. Survey costs
> will be finalized after the ownership frame establishes the number of identifiable owners and the power
> analysis establishes a defensible design.
> Budget rationale
> Survey fielding
> The willingness-to-accept estimates and information-treatment effects depend on the landowner survey.
> Year 1 therefore covers parcel and ownership records, secure survey hosting, individualized parcel maps,
> printing, postage, prepaid incentives, and cognitive interviews. The incentive allocation is provisional.
> After the parcel frame establishes the number of identifiable owners, the power analysis and mailing-cost
> assessment will determine whether the budget supports contacting the full frame or requires a stratified
> sample. Those calculations will also determine the number of invitations and the incentive offered with
> each invitation.
> The survey has four contact waves: a prenotice letter, a main invitation with a prepaid incentive and web
> link, a reminder, and a final invitation with a paper questionnaire. A university mail service or survey
> fulfillment vendor will handle printing, personalization, addressing, packet assembly, postage, return
> envelopes, and mailing. The paper option includes owners who may not use the web instrument. Trained
> personnel will enter and verify returned paper questionnaires under the approved data-security and
> confidentiality protocol. The contingency covers additional fulfillment, incentives, or data-entry costs if
> the completed ownership frame and mailing-cost assessment require them or another follow-up is
> justified. Any change to the contact design will be made before fielding and will remain subject to IRB
> approval.
> Data analysis and decision support
> Spatial data and computing funds cover the parcel frame, nonresponse analysis, and the spatial layer
> delivered to DNREC. Paper questionnaire data entry is placed in Year 2 because final returns may require
> Budget and Timeline | 1
> manual processing. Funds for GIS production and documentation will make the agency-facing layer
> usable and provide reproducible metadata. The project will use DNREC's existing suitability
> classifications and wetland assessments rather than create a separate ecological ranking model.
> Travel and dissemination
> Travel within Delaware covers meetings with DNREC, the Department of Agriculture, the Agricultural
> Lands Preservation Foundation, and landowners who participate in cognitive interviews. Conference
> travel is concentrated in Year 2, after results are available. A final briefing with DNREC staff will focus
> on the parcel frame, the survey estimates, and the assumptions behind the decision scenarios. The budget
> also includes one open-access fee for the manuscript planned during the fellowship.
> Cost controls and adjustment rules
> The survey will include only conservation arrangements that the feasibility review finds legally and
> administratively credible. If agencies can provide parcel records, the amount reserved for commercial
> data will move to survey implementation or data processing. The completed frame will determine whether
> the project contacts all identifiable owners or uses a documented stratified sample. Unused survey funds
> will support response follow-up, processing of paper questionnaires, documentation, or the final DNREC
> briefing, subject to fellowship and host-institution rules.
